<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cotswolds</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cotswolds are a range of limestone hills in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>. Sheep farming made the region rich from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, when its wool was exported across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, leaving a legacy of stone-built market towns and large churches. It was designated an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/area-of-outstanding-natural-beauty">Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty</a> in 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotswolds.jpg" alt="Cotswolds" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saffron Blaze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castle_combe_cotswolds.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
